        }
    })
    .collect::<Vec<_>>()
    ...
}
```

Better approach: for each word, collect the alphabetic characters into a Vec<char>, then take index 0, plus any uppercase letter at index > 0 that comes after a lowercase letter.

```rust
pub fn abbreviate(phrase: &str) -> String {
    phrase
        .split(|c: char| c.is_whitespace() || c == '-')
        .flat_map(|word| {
            word.chars().filter(|c| c.is_alphabetic()).collect::<Vec<_>>()
        })
        .flat_map(|letters| {
            letters.iter().enumerate().filter_map(|(i, c)| {
                if i == 0 || (c.is_uppercase() && letters[i - 1].is_lowercase()) {
                    Some(c.to_uppercase().to_string())
                } else {
                    None
                }
            })
        })
        .collect()
}
```

Wait, but there's a subtle point: "GNU Image Manipulation Program" → "GIMP". "GNU" is all uppercase: the first letter is G, then 'N' after 'G' (uppercase) — not taken, 'U' after 'N' — not taken. Good, "G".

"HyperText": H (first), y, p, e, r, T (uppercase after lowercase r) → T. So "HT". Good.

But wait — what about a word like "iTunes"? i (first), T (uppercase after lowercase i) → "iT" → "IT". That's the expected behavior for the camelcase variant.

Hmm, but there's a subtle issue with the old exercism camelcase test: in the old canonical data, the camelcase test was "HyperText Markup Language" → "HTML". Yes.

Edge case: what if the first character of a word is lowercase and the second is uppercase, e.g., "iT"? i is taken as the first, T is taken after lowercase i → "IT". No problem.

